    \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0432\u0435\u0440\u043d\u0443\u043b \u043c\u0438\u0440."
  interestingFactsEn: "Isaac Newton's life is not a dry story of formulas, but a real tricket with elements of detective, black magic, crazy, and giant ego, and behind this academic genius there was an eccentric, vindictive, obsessive, and very strange man, and below is a collection of the most incredible, curious and shocking facts from his biography, broken by themes.\n\n1. Apple: Truth and fiction The story of the falling apple is the most famous legend in science, but the truth is only half of it.Newton sat by the fireplace or in the garden, saw an apple falling and wondered, \"Why does it fall strictly vertically, not at an angle or up?\" It led him to think that the earth attracts an apple, and the same force might be holding the moon in orbit. - Kurjez: Newton used the apple not for physics, but for optics. He wrote that he often looked at apples in the sun to study theflowers on their skin.--\n\n2. A small pyroman and a list of sins at the age of 19 years, as a student, Newton set up a notebook that recorded all his sins committed in the course of his life (some 58 points in total). This document is the key to his psychosis. The wildest confessions are: \"Did Smith's father and mother burn them and his house above them\" (at the age of three). \"Did my mother die when she was drinking beer.\" \"Beat my sister.\"- \"Eat an apple in a church\" (for a puritanine it was a serious blasphemy). - The obsession with discipline: He punished himself for \"unclean thoughts\" and \"Lein\" but also recorded his scientific insights there, believing that God gave him clues for good behavior.--\n\n3. Experiments over his own vision (Sadomazochism for Science) Newton did more than just observe nature; he tortured himself to understand the device of the eye. His optics read creepy. - Bodkin's needle: Newton took a thin metal needle (Bodkin) and stuck it between his eyeball and the bone of the eye, pressing the back wall of the eye. Then he rotated the needle and watched the color circles change in front of the eyes.He did it not once, but dozens of times, detailing the emerging rainbow spirals. - Experiment with tanning: He looked at the sun through the mirror for a long time to capture the residual images on the retina, which later led to temporary blindness. It took him days to regain his vision, but he did not regret it because he had received data on the nature of the colour acceptance.--\n\n4. Newton's Alchemist spent more time and paper on alchemy than on physics and mathematics combined. His handwritten chemical heritage exceeds a million words.In the 1970s, scientists analyzed the strand of his hair and found in it a mercury concentration greater than the 15–20 times the norm! That explains his insanity attacks (paranoia, memory loss) in 1693. - Shifra: Fearing the inquisition (alchemy in England was prosecuted), Newton was rewriting his recipes into Latin, and sometimes using his ingenious alphabet to make no one understand what he was doing.--\n\n5. The bogus hunter: The judge, jury and executioner, working on the Monet Court, is not a boring bureaucracy. It was a criminal fighter with the participation of a great physicist. - Operational work: Newton changed into plain clothes and personally walked through London taverns and slums, impersonating a drunk Irish merchant to buy fake coins and find a trail of criminals.- Chalonor's execution: The most famous counterfeiter in England, William Chalonor, was witty and arrogant, he challenged Newton openly. Newton collected a file on him for two years and then personally built evidence in court.- Interrogation methods: Newton intimidated the criminals, claiming to have \"mathematic accuracy\" in determining the weight of molten silver, and any deception he reveals to a gram. --\n\n6. Graphoman and egoist: A curved reputation mirror - \"Start\" - is a trick with mass: In its famous \"Mathematical Attractions,\" Newton specifically described it in geometric style (as ancient Greeks), although he himself used his flux (analysis) method to calculate it. Why? So that no one would understand how it came to result.He wrote, \"To avoid mathematician jerk, I'll describe everything geometrically,\" but he really wanted the competitors not to steal his method. Newton's long nose and grudges: Newton was incredibly insulted. He might not have talked to a man for years who dared to question his calculations.So far, we don't know what Robert Hook looked like -- revenge that lasted centuries. --\n\n7. Domestic strangeities are a indifference to food: Newton's dissipation has entered legends. One day, thinking about the task, he boiled his pocket clock instead of an egg, holding it in boiling water and with surprise looking at a \"strange egg.\" Another time, when he was called to lunch, he left the room, but a minded thought turned around and, without reaching the dining room, sat down at the desk, forgetting to eat.- An empty lecture hall: As a professor, Newton often lectured in front of empty benches. The students did not understand his math and simply stopped walking. According to the charter, he had to lecture at least once a week, so he entered an empty audience, turned to the walls and left.\n\n8. Newton &apos; s heretic theorist and predictor of the end of the world was deeply religious, but his faith was heretic. - Antitrinitarian: He denied the doctrine of the Holy Trinity, believing it was a late fiction of Roman popes. He secretly wrote a work proving that the original Bible did not say \"Troits.\" The publication of these works was banned for centuries. - The Apocalypse calculations: Newton dedicated years to deciphering the Book of the Prophet Daniel and the Revelation of John the Bible.He said, \"I say this not to predict time, but to deny the false predictions of fanatics.\" He also calculated that the temple of Jerusalem in ancient times had exact dimensions that encode the laws of the universe.\n\nNewton versus the seafarers and \"Starting\" as poison after the publication of \"The Start\" everyone thought that the book had helped the navigators immediately. However, Newton himself was cool about practical utility. The Halley case: When Edmund Galley asked Newton how astronomers should apply his theory of gravity to navigation, Newton replied with contempt: \"I create teachings for philosophers, not for seafarers.\" He thought applied science was inferior to his dignity.\n\n10. Death and postmortem chickens - Non-participation: When Newton died in March 1727, Newton gave up the assembly (the last communion). The priest insisted, but Newton turned to the wall. For Victorian England, it was so scandalous that the biographers of the century hid the fact. - Where was his brain?: The autopsy showed that he had huge stones in his kidneys (the size of a forest nut) and traces of strong sclerosis in his brain.But neither his brain nor his heart was preserved -- they were just thrown out in embalming, unlike Decart's brain, which was later stolen. - The cosmetic deception: The undertakers, preparing the body for the display in the Westminster Abbey, realized that the face had been badly decomposed. They put a postmortem wax mask on the corpse, painted it with romans, and in this \"pawn\" look Newton was lying in the coffin while the crowds were walking by, thinking they saw a living genius. --\n\n♪ Bones: Newton's strangest experiment was trying to prove that human vision was related to the pressure on eyeballs, but one day he decided to check if music sounds could cause a violin to the eye. He made the servant play the violin while he was looking in the mirror, trying to see how his pupil was vibrating. Of course, he didn't notice anything, but concluded that \"the sound is too weak for retinas.\"Isaac Newton lived a life full of crazy contradictions: he was a puritan and mystic, an ascetic and vindictive intrigue, a genius and a madman, and it was this cocktail that made him a man who turned the world around."
